261: ```css
262: /* [!code filename:CSS] */
263: /* [!code highlight:2] */
264: @layer components {
265:   .card {
266:     background-color: var(--color-white);
267:     border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
268:     padding: --spacing(6);
269:     box-shadow: var(--shadow-xl);
270:   }
271:   /* [!code highlight:2] */
272: }
273: ```
274: 
275: By defining component classes in the `components` layer, you can still use utility classes to override them when necessary:
276: 
277: ```html
278: <!-- [!code filename:HTML] -->
279: <!-- [!code classes:card,rounded-none] -->
280: <!-- Will look like a card, but with square corners -->
281: <div class="card rounded-none">
282:   <!-- ... -->
283: </div>
284: ```
285: 
286: Using Tailwind you probably don't need these types of classes as often as you think. Read our guide on [managing duplication](/docs/styling-with-utility-classes#managing-duplication) for our recommendations.
287: 
288: The `components` layer is also a good place to put custom styles for any third-party components you're using:
289: 
290: ```css
291: /* [!code filename:CSS] */
292: @layer components {
293:   .select2-dropdown {
294:     /* ... */
295:   }
296: }
297: ```
